p>\n\n<h2 id=\"setting-racket-up\">Setting Racket up</h2>\n\n<p>This was pretty easy. Unlike with Guile, OpenBSD has a very up-to-date Racket package, so I just installed that.</p>\n\n<pre><code># pkg_add racket</code></pre>\n\n<p>Now I had a Racket interpreter living at /usr/local/bin/racket. Sure, I could have just run that and started to play around, but I really wanted to install a mode for Emacs, which is what all serious Lisp users do. A quick Google revealed geiser-mode as the premier Scheme interaction mode. Opening up Emacs, I installed that with a quick &ldquo;M-x package-install RET geiser-mode RET&rdquo;, and that was that.</p>\n\n<p>There were a few bindings I had to get used to, but the real interesting part of my experience was using Racket, not my editor (although geiser-mode is pretty good).</p>\n\n<p>After playing around for a bit and installing paredit-mode, I was ready to get started.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"first-impressions\">First impressions</h2>\n\n<p>The first thing you notice when using a Lisp is the large number of parentheses. In Racket, sometimes square brackets are used to clear things up, which really helps. I&rsquo;ve never seen this in Common Lisp or other Schemes, so this must be something Racket-specific.</p>\n\n<div class=\"brush: lisp\">\n <table class=\"sourcetable\">\n  <tbody>\n   <tr>\n    <td class=\"linenos\">\n     <div class=\"linenodiv\">\n      <pre>1\n2\n3</pre></div></td>\n    <td class=\"code\">\n     <div class=\"source\">\n      <pre><span></span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"k\">let</span> <span class=\"p\">((</span><span class=\"nv\">a</span> <span class=\"mi\">5</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n\t  <span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"nv\">b</span> <span class=\"mi\">10</span><span class=\"p\">))</span>\n  <span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"nb\">+</span> <span class=\"nv\">a</span> <span class=\"nv\">b</span><span class=\"p\">))</span>\n</pre></div>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n</div>\n\n<p>That seems pretty trivial and non-threatening, right? Right? Well, it doesn&rsquo;t stay very nice for long unless you use square brackets or some other distinct type of bracket.</p>\n\n<div class=\"brush: lisp\">\n <table class=\"sourcetable\">\n  <tbody>\n   <tr>\n    <td class=\"linenos\">\n     <div class=\"linenodiv\">\n      <pre>1\n2\n3</pre></div></td>\n    <td class=\"code\">\n     <div class=\"source\">\n      <pre><span></span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"k\">let</span> <span class=\"p\">((</span><span class=\"nv\">a</span> <span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"nb\">map</span> <span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"k\">lambda</span> <span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"nv\">x</span><span class=\"p\">)</span> <span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"nv\">equal?</span> <span class=\"nv\">x</span> <span class=\"s\">\"hello\"</span><span class=\"p\">))))</span>\n\t  <span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"nv\">b</span> <span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"nv\">get-field</span> <span class=\"nv\">info</span> <span class=\"nv\">some-struct</span><span class=\"p\">)))</span>\n<span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"nb\">map</span> <span class=\"nb\">list</span> <span class=\"nv\">a</span> <span class=\"nv\">b</span><span class=\"p\">))</span>\n</pre></div>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n</div>\n\n<p>Maybe you don&rsquo;t think that&rsquo;s too bad, but it looks a lot better with square brackets. Mainly, it lets you keep track of which parens close with expressions. Even with rainbow-delimiters mode, it&rsquo;s really difficult! It&rsquo;s a bit less difficult in Racket, though.</p>\n\n<p>Coming from Haskell, it was a bit jarring to see that there was no type safety at all in default Racket. You could have something like this:</p>\n\n<div class=\"brush: lisp\">\n <table class=\"sourcetable\">\n  <tbody>\n   <tr>\n    <td class=\"linenos\">\n     <div class=\"linenodiv\">\n      <pre>1\n2\n3</pre></div></td>\n    <td class=\"code\">\n     <div class=\"source\">\n      <pre><span></span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"k\">if</span> <span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"nv\">equal?</span> <span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"nb\">read-line</span><span class=\"p\">)</span> <span class=\"s\">\"error out please\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n  <span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"nb\">read-line</span> <span class=\"mi\">4</span> <span class=\"mi\">5</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n  <span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"nb\">+</span> <span class=\"mi\">3</span> <span class=\"mi\">4</span><span class=\"p\">))</span>\n</pre></div>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n</div>\n\n<p>Of course, since <code>(read-line)</code> doesn&rsquo;t take any arguments, you&rsquo;ll get an error at runtime complaining about giving it the wrong number of arguments. But that&rsquo;s the point, you only get the error at <em>runtime</em>. In fact, if you never type in &ldquo;error out please&rdquo; you&rsquo;ll <em>never</em> get an error. Oh man, that&rsquo;s scary: you&rsquo;re supposed to catch all errors at compile-time with a good type system.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"typed-racket\">Typed Racket</h2>\n\n<p>In Racket, you start every file with a declaration of what language you&rsquo;re using. Usually:</p>\n\n<pre><code>#lang racket</code></pre>\n\n<p>But sometimes you might be writing a document or info file for raco, so you&rsquo;ll want a different one. Here&rsquo;s one I always use, especially after that horror story about runtime errors:</p>\n\n<pre><code>#lang typed/racket</code></pre>\n\n<p>Pretty self-explanatory, I think. Here&rsquo;s how some normal Racket code might look:</p>\n\n<div class=\"brush: lisp\">\n <table class=\"sourcetable\">\n  <tbody>\n   <tr>\n    <td class=\"linenos\">\n     <div class=\"linenodiv\">\n      <pre>1\n2\n3</pre></div></td>\n    <td class=\"code\">\n     <div class=\"source\">\n      <pre><span></span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"nv\">define</span> <span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"nv\">square</span> <span class=\"nv\">n</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n  <span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"nb\">*</span> <span class=\"nv\">n</span> <span class=\"nv\">n</span><span class=\"p\">))</span>\n<span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"nv\">define</span> <span class=\"nv\">my-numbers</span> <span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"nb\">list</span> <span class=\"mi\">1</span> <span class=\"mi\">2</span> <span class=\"mi\">5</span> <span class=\"mi\">3</span> <span class=\"mi\">8</span> <span class=\"mi\">5</span><span class=\"p\">))</span>\n</pre></div>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n</div>\n\n<p>In Typed Racket, you can put in type annotations, too! It&rsquo;s usually pretty easy to see what&rsquo;s meant. Here are the appropriate type annotations for the two bindings above:</p>\n\n<div class=\"brush: lisp\">\n <table class=\"sourcetable\">\n  <tbody>\n   <tr>\n    <td class=\"linenos\">\n     <div class=\"linenodiv\">\n      <pre>1\n2</pre></div></td>\n    <td class=\"code\">\n     <div class=\"source\">\n      <pre><span></span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"err\">:</span> <span class=\"nv\">square</span> <span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"nv\">-&gt;</span> <span class=\"nv\">Number</span> <span class=\"nv\">Number</span><span class=\"p\">))</span>\n<span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"err\">:</span> <span class=\"nv\">my-numbers</span> <span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"nv\">Listof</span> <span class=\"nv\">Integer</span><span class=\"p\">))</span>\n</pre></div>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n</div>\n\n<p>Those actually do look similar to type annotations in Haskell:</p>\n\n<div class=\"brush: haskell\">\n <table class=\"sourcetable\">\n  <tbody>\n   <tr>\n    <td class=\"linenos\">\n     <div class=\"linenodiv\">\n      <pre>1\n2</pre></div></td>\n    <td class=\"code\">\n     <div class=\"source\">\n      <pre><span></span><span class=\"nf\">square</span> <span class=\"ow\">::</span> <span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"kt\">Num</span> <span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"p\">)</span> <span class=\"ow\">=&gt;</span> <span class=\"n\">a</span> <span class=\"ow\">-&gt;</span> <span class=\"n\">a</span>\n<span class=\"nf\">myNumbers</span> <span class=\"ow\">::</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"kt\">Int</span><span class=\"p\">]</span>\n</pre></div>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n</div>\n\n<p>Personally, I like the Haskell syntax more. Of course, since Racket is a Lisp, the syntax can be changed infinitely, so that&rsquo;s not really a complaint.</p>\n\n<p>All in all, the Racket REPL is cool (although nothing special nowadays), the language is good if unsafe, Typed Racket is nicer, Haskell, ML, OCaml and other &ldquo;real&rdquo; functional languages still feel nicer to me, though.</p>\n\n<p>A few days isn&rsquo;t enough to really get to know a language, though, so maybe I&rsquo;m wrong. Until someone tells me so, I won&rsquo;t use any Lisp as extensively as I do Haskell, mostly because it feels unsafe.</p>") #f (u . "\n<p>I&rsquo;ve heard a lot of things about Racket (well really, things about many different Lisp dialects), and most of them were good. Recently, I decided to try to decide between Haskell and a Lisp once and for all. I wanted to go for a Lisp&ndash;1, since they keep functions and values in the same namespace, which is how it <em>should</em> be. Eventually, after trying out Guile, Chicken Scheme, and a few others, I settled on Racket.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"setting-racket-up\">Setting Racket up</h2>\n\n<p>This was pretty easy. Unlike with Guile, OpenBSD has a very up-to-date Racket package, so I just installed that.</p>\n\n<pre><code># pkg_add racket</code></pre>\n\n<p>Now I had a Racket interpreter living at /usr/local/bin/racket. Sure, I could have just run that and started to play around, but I really wanted to install a mode for Emacs, which is what all serious Lisp users do. A quick Google revealed geiser-mode as the premier Scheme interaction mode. Open