ind that having to build from source and not being able to manage dependencies is the worst of every single world: installing anything takes ages and the builds will all probably fail halfway through.</p>\n\n<p>Of course, there is a &ldquo;solution&rdquo; in place: queues. You&rsquo;re supposed to look up the whole dependency tree beforehand and queue up all of the packages, one by one. Pretty tedious.</p>\n\n<p>There are alternatives, like slapt-get, but those require you to surrender all control to the package manager, at which point you might as well go back to Ubuntu. So we want dependency management and control. Where can we turn? Of course, NetBSD&rsquo;s pkgsrc.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"getting-started\">Getting started</h2>\n\n<p>You might think that since it&rsquo;s part of the NetBSD project, pkgsrc can only run on NetBSD. That&rsquo;s not true, it can run on any vaguely POSIXy OS. To get started on Slackware, you need to make sure you have a few of the sets. I&rsquo;m not sure how many you actually need, but this is what I got it down to:</p>\n\n<ul>\n <li>a</li>\n <li>ap</li>\n <li>d</li>\n <li>l</li>\n <li>n</li></ul>\n\n<p>The need for &ldquo;a&rdquo; is obvious: that&rsquo;s the base. You need &ldquo;d&rdquo; and &ldquo;l&rdquo; for compiling, which we&rsquo;ll be doing a lot of. &ldquo;n&rdquo; for downloading pkgsrc. &ldquo;ap&rdquo; for, well, being able to do things while you build everything (which could take a while). Eventually, you could uninstall &ldquo;ap&rdquo; and &ldquo;n&rdquo; and replace everything with programs from the pkgsrc tree. You can&rsquo;t do that yet, though.</p>\n\n<p>Next, get and extract the pkgsrc tree:</p>\n\n<div class=\"brush: bash\">\n <table class=\"sourcetable\">\n  <tbody>\n   <tr>\n    <td class=\"linenos\">\n     <div class=\"linenodiv\">\n      <pre>1\n2\n3</pre></div></td>\n    <td class=\"code\">\n     <div class=\"source\">\n      <pre><span></span><span class=\"c1\"># cd /usr</span>\n<span class=\"c1\"># wget ftp://ftp.netbsd.org/pub/pkgsrc/current/pkgsrc.tar.gz</span>\n<span class=\"c1\"># tar xzvf pkgsrc.tar.gz</span>\n</pre></div>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n</div>\n\n<p>Now, there is a ports-looking sort of thing under /usr/pkgsrc. Nifty. But you need NetBSD&rsquo;s make to use it. Luckily, there&rsquo;s a bootstrap script to get you started.</p>\n\n<div class=\"brush: bash\">\n <table class=\"sourcetable\">\n  <tbody>\n   <tr>\n    <td class=\"linenos\">\n     <div class=\"linenodiv\">\n      <pre>1\n2</pre></div></td>\n    <td class=\"code\">\n     <div class=\"source\">\n      <pre><span></span><span class=\"c1\"># cd /usr/pkgsrc/bootstrap</span>\n<span class=\"c1\"># ./bootstrap</span>\n</pre></div>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n</div>\n\n<p>Something should happen and at the end, there should be some binaries in /usr/pkg/bin and /usr/pkg/sbin. That&rsquo;s where all of the pkgsrc installed stuff will go, so you might want to add the following to your shell&rsquo;s profile:</p>\n\n<div class=\"brush: bash\">\n <table class=\"sourcetable\">\n  <tbody>\n   <tr>\n    <td class=\"linenos\">\n     <div class=\"linenodiv\">\n      <pre>1</pre></div></td>\n    <td class=\"code\">\n     <div class=\"source\">\n      <pre><span></span><span class=\"nb\">export</span> <span class=\"nv\">PATH</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"nv\">$PATH</span>:/usr/pkg/bin:/usr/pkg/sbin\n</pre></div>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n</div>\n\n<p>Additionally, you might want man pages.</p>\n\n<div class=\"brush: bash\">\n <table class=\"sourcetable\">\n  <tbody>\n   <tr>\n    <td class=\"linenos\">\n     <div class=\"linenodiv\">\n      <pre>1</pre></div></td>\n    <td class=\"code\">\n     <div class=\"source\">\n      <pre><span></span><span class=\"nb\">export</span> <span class=\"nv\">MANPATH</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"nv\">$MANPATH</span>:/usr/pkg/man\n</pre></div>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n</div>\n\n<p>Now everything&rsquo;s set up to start building. You might not have an X server yet and even if you do, you&rsquo;ll want to replace it with pkgsrc&rsquo;s. Installing a port is easy:</p>\n\n<div class=\"brush: bash\">\n <table class=\"sourcetable\">\n  <tbody>\n   <tr>\n    <td class=\"linenos\">\n     <div class=\"linenodiv\">\n      <pre>1\n2</pre></div></td>\n    <td class=\"code\">\n     <div class=\"source\">\n      <pre><span></span>$ <span class=\"nb\">cd</span> /usr/pkgsrc/x11/modular-xorg-server\n$ sudo bmake install\n</pre></div>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n</div>\n\n<p>On my old laptop, this took several hours. If you have a more recent machine, it shouldn&rsquo;t take too long.</p>\n\n<p>While building, you might think &ldquo;Hey, why&rsquo;s it installing Perl, I already have that!&rdquo;. That&rsquo;s a valid question, but pkgsrc doesn&rsquo;t detect dependencies by looking in /usr/bin/, it searches its own package database, which is completely empty.</p>\n\n<p>This means that you&rsquo;ll end up building Perl, Python and dozens of libraries you already have. After pkgsrc is done, you might as well uninstall all of the Slackware packages you don&rsquo;t need. Go to /var/log/packages and and <code>removepkg</code> everything you don&rsquo;t want.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"what-now\">What now?</h2>\n\n<p>You can search the ports tree with /usr/pkgsrc/pkglocate for all your favourite software and install it. There&rsquo;s more software in pkgsrc than there is in the default Slackware repos and it&rsquo;s a lot more convenient than sbopkg. For more info: <a href=\"http://www.netbsd.org/docs/pkgsrc/\">http://www.netbsd.org/docs/pkgsrc/</a>.</p>\n\n<p>Disclaimer: I do not use pkgsrc on Slackware on a day-to-day basis, so don&rsquo;t blame me for your problems. Send something in to the pkgsrc mailing list or some NetBSD IRC channel instead.</p>") #f (u . "\n<p>Back in the day, I used to use Slackware. It was the best distro around and all the cool kids used it. Nowadays, it&rsquo;s rather different: a much lower proportion of