, I lost the old one, and revoked the other one&hellip;due to losing it. Do what I say, not what I do.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"summary\">Summary</h2>\n\n<p><code>gpg -ear $KEY</code> - Encrypt plaintext from stdin</p>\n\n<p><code>gpg -d</code> - Decrypt plaintext from stdin</p>\n\n<p><code>gpg --export -a $KEY</code> - Export ASCII-armoured key to stdout</p>\n\n<p><code>gpg --import $FILE</code> - Import key from a file</p>\n\n<p><code>gpg --clearsign</code> - Sign a message from stdin, leaving the message human-readable</p>\n\n<p><code>gpg --detach-sig $SIGFILE $FILE</code> - Sign a file and create a detached signature in another file</p>\n\n<p><code>gpg --some-sort-of-command --output $FILE</code> - Do something, then output to a file</p>") #f (u . "\n<h2 id=\"why-am-i-writing-this\">Why am I writing this?</h2>\n\n<p>I have looked up &ldquo;how to use gpg&rdquo; so many times, on so many websites, and have found every guide to be focused on something I don&rsquo;t use or worded in such a way that I get confused and revoke all of my keys (that hasn&rsquo;t actually happened&hellip;yet). I thought I&rsquo;d whip up a quick guide that could serve as a reference for future Kaashif, who may not remember anything about GPG other than <code>gpg -ear</code> and <code>gpg -d</code>.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"installing-gpg\">Installing GPG</h2>\n\n<p>This is easy. Most distros come with it, for package signing among other things. The ones that don&rsquo;t have it easily installable from their package repos as either &ldquo;gpg&rdquo;, &ldquo;gpg2&rdquo;, &ldquo;gnupg&rdquo; or &ldquo;gnupg2&rdquo;. While GPG and GPG 2 are actually different programs, many distros don&rsquo;t make the distinction, since hardly anyone uses GPG1 anymore.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"generating-a-key\">Generating a key</h2>\n\n<p><code>gpg --gen-key</code> You have to be an idiot to get this wrong. Defaults are fine, unless someone has broken RSA with quantum magic. Make sure the email is right.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"after-genera