04/19/iodine-is-cool/index.html" . unix) "/2015/04/19/iodine-is-cool/" (u . "2015-04-19T00:00:00") (? . 7) (? . 35) (c (u . "")) (u . "\n<p>I know that sometimes, I&rsquo;ve bene stuck in an airport or in a coffee shop without internet. That&rsquo;s annoying in and of itself, but it&rsquo;s even more annoying when there&rsquo;s a WiFi hotspot nearby, but it requires you to pay £4/hour or something crazy like that.</p>\n\n<p>You can still connect to the network, it&rsquo;s just that whatever URL you visit in your browser, you get redirected to the &ldquo;pay for internet&rdquo; page. Obviously, you can&rsquo;t SSH tunnel somewhere, or use an ordinary HTTP proxy, those things are all blocked.</p>\n\n<p>In desperation, I would ping google.com, since that&rsquo;s what everyone does to check internet connectivity. There, I noticed something:</p>\n\n<pre><code>$ ping google.com\nPING google.com (216.58.210.46) 56(84) bytes of data.</code></pre>\n\n<p>How did it know the IP of google.com? It must have resolved it using a DNS server, it&rsquo;s not like I have a big list of sites in my /etc/hosts.</p>\n\n<p>That means I can request information from a server, and get back information from on any server on the internet. All it would take is someone running a DNS server (or similar) putting some data in the records it sends back and I&rsquo;ll have a way of communicating to the outside&hellip; without even logging into the hotspot.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"iodine---how-to-install-and-set-up\">Iodine - how to install and set up</h2>\n\n<p>I was ready to write a solution myself, but it already existed. Iodine! Here&rsquo;s how to set it up.</p>\n\n<p>First, set up an A record pointing to your public server and an NS record pointing to that A record. Basically set it up like the <a href=\"http://code.kryo.se/iodine/README.html\">readme</a> tells you to.</p>\n\n<p>Now, on the server pointed to by the A record (assuming Debian):</p>\n\n<pre><code>$ sudo apt install iodine</code></pre>\n\n<p>Obviously that&rsquo;s not going to do anything. On Debian, there is a config file at /etc/default/iodine. Fire up your editor and open it, and make the relevant variables look like this:</p>\n\n<pre><code>IODINED_ARGS=\"-c -d tap0 192.168.233.1/24 t1.mydomain.com\"\nIODINED_PASSWORD=\"mypassword\"</code></pre>\n\n<p>Where t1.mydomain.com is an NS record, like in the README. Put in something secure for the password. Now, all you need to do is start it up:</p>\n\n<pre><code>$ sudo systemctl start iodined</code></pre>\n\n<p>And the server should be running. Check <code>systemctl status iodined</code> for any errors if you&rsquo;re really paranoid.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"the-client\">The client</h2>\n\n<p>On the client, setup is just as easy. To make your life a bit easier, why not make iodine a service? Install iodine and edit /etc/systemd/system/iodineclient.service and put in the following:</p>\n\n<pre><code>[Unit]\nDescription=Iodine DNS proxy\n\n[Service]\nExecStart=/usr/sbin/iodine -fP mypassword t1.mydomain.com\n\n[Install]\nWantedBy=multi-user.target</code></pre>\n\n<p>Now, just <code>systemctl start iodineclient</code> and wait a while for it to connect and you&rsquo;ll be golden. You&rsquo;ll get an IP of 192.168.233.2, if you used the same settings I did. The server it&rsquo;s running from should be at 192.168.233.1, so you can run whatever web proxy you want there, and you&rsquo;ll be able to access it so long as you can query DNS servers for t1.mydomain.com.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"setting-up-squid\">Setting up Squid</h2>\n\n<p>The whole point of this was to access the web from access points where all I have is DNS, so I need a web proxy. I chose Squid. Setting it up is <em>really</em> simple. On the server:</p>\n\n<pre><code>$ sudo apt install squid\n$ echo 'http_access allow localnet' | sudo tee -a /etc/squid/squid.conf\n$ sudo systemctl enable squid\n$ sudo systemctl start squid</code></pre>\n\n<p>I know there are a lot of other things you can do with Squid, but with this, you can just fire up iodine and use 192.168.233.1 on port 3128 as your HTTP proxy and it&rsquo;ll be like you paid that £400/second to access the internet, but your wallet will stay full.</p>") #f (u . "\n<p>I know that sometimes, I&rsquo;ve bene stuck in an airport or in a coffee shop without internet. That&rsquo;s annoying in and of itself, but it&rsquo;s even more annoying when there&rsquo;s a WiFi hotspot nearby, but it requires you to pay £4/hour or something crazy like that.</p>\n\n<p>You can still connect to the network, it&rsquo;s just that whatever URL you visit in your browser, you get redirected to the &ldquo;pay for internet&rdquo; page. Obviously, you can&rsquo;t SSH tunnel somewhere, or use an ordinary HTTP proxy, those things are all blocked.</p>\n\n<p>In desperation, I would ping google.com, since that&rsquo;s what everyone does to check internet connectivity. There, I noticed something:</p>\n\n<pre><code>$ ping google.com\nPING google.com (216.58.210.46) 56(84) bytes of data.</code></pre>\n\n<p>How did it know the IP of google.com? It must have resolved it using a DNS server, it&rsquo;s not like I have a big list of sites in my /etc/hosts.</p>\n\n<p>That means I can request information from a server, and get back information from on any server on the internet. All it would take is someone running a DNS server (or similar) putting some data in the records it sends back and I&rsquo;ll have a way of communicating to the outside&hellip; without even logging into the hotspot.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"iodine---how-to-install-and-set-up\">Iodine - how to install and set up</h2>\n\n<p>I was ready to write a solution myself, but it already existed. Iodine! Here&rsquo;s how to set it up.</p>\n\n<p>First, set up an A record pointing to your public server and an NS record pointing to that A record. Basically set it up like the <a href=\"http://code.kryo.se/iodine/README.html\">readme</a> tells you to.</p>\n\n<p>Now, on the server pointed to by the A record (assuming Debian):</p>\n\n<pre><code>$ sudo apt install iodine</code></pre>\n\n<p>Obviously that&rsquo;s not going to do anything. On Debian, there is a config file at /etc/default/iodine. Fire up your editor and open it, and make the relevant variables look like this:</p>\n\n<pre><code>IODINED_ARGS=\"-c -d tap0 192.168.233.1/24 t1.mydomain.com\"\nIODINED_PASSWORD=\"mypassword\"</code></pre>\n\n<p>Where t1.mydomain.com is an NS record, like in the README. Put in something secure for the password. Now, all you need to do is start it up:</p>\n\n<pre><code>$ sudo systemctl start iodined</code></pre>\n\n<p>And the server should be running. Check <code>systemctl status iodined</code> for any errors if you&rsquo;re really paranoid.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"the-client\">The client</h2>\n\n<p>On the client, setup is just as easy. To make your life a bit easier, why not make iodine a service? Install iodine and edit /etc/systemd/system/iodineclient.service and put in the following:</p>\n\n<pre><code>[Unit]\nDescription=Iodine DNS proxy\n\n[Service]\nExecStart=/usr/sbin/iodine -fP mypassword t1.mydomain.com\n\n[Install]\nWantedBy=multi-user.target</code></pre>\n\n<p>Now, just <code>systemctl start iodineclient</code> and wait a while for it to connect and you&rsquo;ll be golden. You&rsquo;ll get an IP of 192.168.233.2, if you used the same settings I did. The server it&rsquo;s running from should be at 192.168.233.1, so you can run whatever web proxy you want there, and you&rsquo;ll be able to access it so long as you can query DNS servers for t1.mydomain.com.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"setting-up-squid\">Setting up Squid</h2>\n\n<p>The whole point of this was to access the web from access points where all I have is DNS, so I need a web proxy. I chose Squid. Setting it up is <em>really</em> simple. On the server:</p>\n\n<pre><code>$ sudo apt install squid\n$ echo 'http_access allow localnet' | sudo tee -a /etc/squid/squid.conf\n$ sudo systemctl enable squid\n$ sudo systemctl start squid</code></pre>\n\n<p>I know there are a lot of other things you can do with Squid, but with this, you can just fire up iodine and use 192.168.233.1 on port 3128 as your HTTP proxy and it&rsquo;ll be like you paid that £400/second to access the internet, but your wallet will stay full.</p>")) ((p+ #"/home/kaashif/src/blog/frog/_src/posts/2015-06-18-kvm.md" . unix) f post (u . "How to get a list of processes on OpenBSD (in C)") (? . 35) 1461781114 (p+ #"/home/kaashif/src/blog/frog/_out/2015/06/18/how-to-get-a-list-of-processes-on-openbsd-in-c/index.html" . unix) "/2015/06/18/how-to-get-a-list-of-processes-on-openbsd-in-c/" (u . "2015-06-18T00:00:00") (? . 34) (? . 36) (c (u . "")) (u . "\n<h2 id=\"is-it-portable\">Is it portable?</h2>\n\n<p>First off, the information in this post definitely doesn&rsquo;t apply to Linux (as it has a completely different way of doing things) and may or may not apply to other BSDs (I see that NetBSD and FreeBSD both have similar, maybe identical, kvm(3) interfaces). There certainly isn&rsquo;t anything in POSIX to make this standard. The only real reason any UNIX-like OSes have this particular interface comes from the kvm(3) man page in OpenBSD:</p>\n\n<blockquote>\n <p>The kvm interface was first introduced in SunOS. A considerable number of programs have been developed that use this interface, making backward compatibility highly desirable. In most respects, the Sun kvm interface is consistent and clean. Accordingly, the generic portion of the interface (i.e., kvm_open(), kvm_close(), kvm_read(), kvm_write(), and kvm_nlist()) has been incorporated into the BSD interface. Indeed, many kvm applications (i.e., debuggers and statistical monitors) use only this subset of the interface.</p></blockquote>\n\n<p>So even with that, only the &ldquo;generic portion&rdquo; of the interface is &ldquo;standardised&rdquo; (although it&rsquo;s not <em>really</em> standardised, it&rsquo;s just de facto). Hence, using kvm_openfiles(3) and the like has no reason to work on any other OS the same way it does on OpenBSD.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"actually-writing-some-code\">Actually writing some code</h2>\n\n<p>The information about running processes is stored somewhere: the kernel. Even in Linux, with procfs (/proc), the info all really comes from the kernel.</p>\n\n<p>On OpenBSD, you want to access the running system&rsquo;s kernel image. This is done using kvm_openfiles(3), which takes a host of parameters detailing the <em>file</em> you want to load the kernel image from. Obviously, you don&rsquo;t want to load a file, but the running kernel. To do this, just pass in NULL as the parameters that have anything to do with files: the function will know this means you want the running system:</p>\n\n<div class=\"brush: c\">\n <table class=\"sourcetable\">\n  <tbody>\n   <tr>\n    <td class=\"linenos\">\n     <div class=\"linenodiv\">\n      <pre>1\n2\n3\n4\n5\n6\n7\n8\n9</pre></div></td>\n    <td class=\"code\">\n     <div class=\"source\">\n      <pre><span></span><span class=\"cp\">#include</span> <span class=\"cpf\">&lt;stdio.h&gt;</span><span class=\"cp\"></span>\n<span class=\"cp\">#include</span> <span class=\"cpf\">&lt;kvm.h&gt;</span><span class=\"cp\"></span>\n<span class=\"cp\">#include</span> <span class=\"cpf\">&lt;limits.h&gt;</span><span class=\"cp\"></span>\n\n<span class=\"kt\">int</span>\n<span class=\"nf\">main</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"kt\">void</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"p\">{</span>\n    <span class=\"kt\">char</span> <span class=\"n\">errbuf</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"n\">_POSIX2_LINE_MAX</span><span class=\"p\">];</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">kvm_t</span> <span class=\"o\">*</span><span class=\"n\">kernel</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">kvm_openfiles</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"nb\">NULL</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"nb\">NULL</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"nb\">NULL</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">KVM_NOFILES</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">errbuf</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n</pre></div>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n</div>\n\n<p>You should really check if it&rsquo;s null or whatever, but for didactic purposes, I&rsquo;ll just leave out all of the boring error handling - it&rsquo;s obvious where the error string would be stored (hint - errbuf), so you can handle that.</p>\n\n<p>Next, you want to get a list of processes from that kernel image using kvm_getprocs(3):</p>\n\n<div class=\"brush: c\">\n <table class=\"sourcetable\">\n  <tbody>\n   <tr>\n    <td class=\"linenos\">\n     <div class=\"linenodiv\">\n      <pre>1\n2</pre></div></td>\n    <td class=\"code\">\n     <div class=\"source\">\n      <pre><span></span>    <span class=\"kt\">int</span> <span class=\"n\">nentries</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">;</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">struct</span> <span class=\"n\">kinfo_proc</span> <span class=\"o\">*</span><span class=\"n\">kinfo</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">kvm_getprocs</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">kernel</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">KERN_PROC_ALL</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"k\">sizeof</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"o\">*</span><span class=\"n\">kernel</span><span class=\"p\">),</span> <span class=\"o\">&amp;</span><span class=\"n\">nentries</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n</pre></div>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n</div>\n\n<p>Again, check if null, handle errors. The number of processes obtained is stored in nentries. If you&rsquo;re wondering, the &ldquo;0&rdquo; in the arguments to kvm_getprocs(3) actually doesn&rsquo;t matter - KERN_PROC_ALL is an operation that doesn&rsquo;t take an argument. There are other useful operations which do take an argument, so see kvm_getprocs(3) for info on those.</p>\n\n<p>Now, you obviously want to go through the processes and do something with them: you know how many processes there are and you have the pointer to the first one, so a simple for loop with a counter will do. Why don&rsquo;t we just print the binary name for every process?</p>\n\n<div class=\"brush: c\">\n <table class=\"sourcetable\">\n  <tbody>\n   <tr>\n    <td class=\"linenos\">\n     <div class=\"linenodiv\">\n      <pre>1\n2\n3\n4</pre></div></td>\n    <td class=\"code\">\n     <div class=\"source\">\n      <pre><span></span>    <span class=\"kt\">int</span> <span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"p\">;</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">for</span> <span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">i</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">;</span> <span class=\"n\">i</span> <span class=\"o\">&lt;</span> <span class=\"n\">nentries</span><span class=\"p\">;</span> <span class=\"o\">++</span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"p\">)</span> <span class=\"p\">{</span>\n        <span class=\"n\">printf</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"%s</span><span class=\"se\">\\n</span><span class=\"s\">\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">kinfo</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"p\">].</span><span class=\"n\">p_comm</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n    <span class=\"p\">}</span>\n</pre></div>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n</div>\n\n<p>Now that was easy. There are a lot of fields in the kinfo_proc struct, and there actually isn&rsquo;t a man page for them, since the full definition is available in &lt;sys/sysctl.h&gt;. Look there (/usr/include/sys/sysctl.h) for info on information you can get.</p>\n\n<p>Almost forgot: this is supposed to be a valid program, so return something:</p>\n\n<div class=\"brush: c\">\n <table class=\"sourcetable\">\n  <tbody>\n   <tr>\n    <td class=\"linenos\">\n     <div class=\"linenodiv\">\n      <pre>1\n2</pre></div></td>\n    <td class=\"code\">\n     <div class=\"source\">\n      <pre><span></span>    <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">;</span>\n<span class=\"p\">}</span>\n</pre></div>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n</div>\n\n<h2 id=\"see-also\">See Also</h2>\n\n<p>This short tutorial is basically just a gateway into the hundreds of possible things you can do with processes in OpenBSD. The documentation in the man pages is excellent, so don&rsquo;t hesitate to use apropos(1) in your search for more knowledge. Also, you can just go to the man page for kvm(3) and just work your way through the &ldquo;SEE ALSO&rdquo; sections, playing with functions you find as you go.</p>\n\n<p>Have fun!</p>") #f (u . "\n<h2 id=\"is-it-portable\">Is it portable?</h2>\n\n<p>First off, the information in this post definitely doesn&rsquo;t apply to Linux (as it has a completely different way of doing things) and may or may not apply to other BSDs (I see that NetBSD and FreeBSD both have similar, maybe identical, kvm(3) interfaces). There certainly isn&rsquo;t anything in POSIX to make this standard. The only real reason any UNIX-like OSes have this particular interface comes from the kvm(3) man page in OpenBSD:</p>\n\n<blockquote>\n <p>The kvm interface was first introduced in SunOS. A considerable number of programs have been developed that use this interface, making backward compatibility highly desirable. In most respects, the Sun kvm interface is consistent and clean. Accordingly, the generic portion of the interface (i.e., kvm_open(), kvm_close(), kvm_read(), kvm_write(), and kvm_nlist()) has been incorporated into the BSD interface. Indeed, many kvm applications (i.e., debuggers and statistical monitors) use only this subset of the interface.</p></blockquote>\n\n<p>So even with that, only the &ldquo;generic portion&rdquo; of the interface is &ldquo;standardised&rdquo; (although it&rsquo;s not <em>really</em> standardised, it&rsquo;s just de facto). Hence, using kvm_openfiles(3)